WEBVTT

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:02.410
Hi, I'm Tuba!

00:00:02.410 --> 00:00:07.264
Looped transformers are usually compared at equal parameters, while quietly spending more compute.

00:00:07.264 --> 00:00:09.504
SMELT loops and pays the bill.

00:00:09.504 --> 00:00:10.250
Here's how.

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:15.669
The idea: loop the middle layers, then pay for the extra passes.

00:00:15.669 --> 00:00:20.078
A narrower width cuts the compute, and more experts buy the parameters back.

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:26.071
Take a twelve-layer mixture of experts, and loop its middle six layers twice.

00:00:26.071 --> 00:00:28.885
Eighteen layers execute, from twelve layers of weights.

00:00:29.305 --> 00:00:32.920
Inside the loop, each pass writes to the residual stream at half

00:00:32.920 --> 00:00:35.029
strength, so repeated visits don't inflate it.

00:00:35.449 --> 00:00:36.816
Why only the middle?

00:00:36.816 --> 00:00:40.919
The first and last layers do specialized work that repetition doesn't improve.

00:00:40.919 --> 00:00:43.312
And twice beats three or four times.

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:50.000
Left alone, looping six extra layers is a one point five times compute tax.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:53.358
A fair comparison has to hold three budgets at once.

00:00:53.778 --> 00:00:57.506
Narrow the hidden width, from twelve eighty to ten fifty-six, and the

00:00:57.506 --> 00:00:59.992
extra passes fit the same compute per token.

00:01:00.412 --> 00:01:04.229
That loses parameters, so widen the expert pool, from a hundred and

00:01:04.229 --> 00:01:06.773
ninety-two to two hundred and eighty-eight per layer.

00:01:07.193 --> 00:01:10.866
Head size and the GQA ratio hold the KV cache level.

00:01:10.866 --> 00:01:13.203
All three budgets land within four percent.

00:01:15.050 --> 00:01:18.892
Matched on all three, looping still wins: six point eight to eighteen

00:01:18.892 --> 00:01:21.454
percent less training compute for the same loss.

00:01:21.454 --> 00:01:23.055
Real, but no free lunch.

00:01:24.850 --> 00:01:28.421
Looping is a real architectural lever, not just relabeled scale.

00:01:28.421 --> 00:01:31.634
Just a smaller one, once somebody bothers to check.

00:01:33.275 --> 00:01:37.095
To recap: loop the middle twice, trade width for experts to keep

00:01:37.095 --> 00:01:39.961
every budget level, and bank a modest, real saving.

00:01:40.381 --> 00:01:42.508
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:42.508 --> 00:01:43.116
I'm Tuba.

00:01:43.116 --> 00:01:43.420
Bye!
